2026-09-28T22:41:00-07:00 FLAW   DESIGN NOTE SECTION 0/4 IS INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT. It pairs "3 pclk/command, 16 B/command" with a 16-bit cmd_data port plus an 8-word write combiner, and still claims 531 MB/s. A 16-bit port supplies 2 B/pclk. 16 B every 3 pclk CONSUMES 5.3333 B/pclk. The app can feed 1/8 of what the DRAM cadence drains, so the combiner empties and the queue starves. The 531 MB/s claim is unreachable with that port, by a factor of 2.0088x -- it is not merely optimistic, it is off by 2x and in the wrong direction.
2026-09-28T22:41:05-07:00 FLAW   Ceilings at pclk = 99.5625e6, stated as a rate with both factors per AGENTS.md 3:
                        16-bit cmd_data (2 B/cmd, 1 cmd/pclk)   ceiling  199.125 MB/s
                        64-bit cmd_data (8 B/cmd, 1 cmd/pclk)   ceiling  796.500 MB/s
                        128-bit cmd_data (16 B/cmd, 1 cmd/pclk) ceiling 1593.000 MB/s
                      400 MB/s requires 4.0176 B/pclk offered. Only the 64-bit and 128-bit ports can offer it.
2026-09-28T22:41:10-07:00 FLAW   CONSEQUENCE FOR THE PLAN. The write combiner is not needed at all. One 128-bit command IS one 16 B BL8 burst, so a 128-bit port makes the consumer's per-command payload and the DRAM's per-command payload the same width: the port feeds 1 cmd/pclk (1593 MB/s ceiling) and the issue engine drains at 1 cmd / 3 pclk (531 MB/s). The queue never starves. This REMOVES the 8-word combiner, the misaligned/BC4 fallback and the ordering hazard the note warned about, and it is strictly less RTL.
2026-09-28T22:41:15-07:00 FLAW   The note's own measured baseline agrees with this: VERIFY_BLOCK is already 16 B/cmd READ at 12 pclk/cmd = 132.750 MB/s, and 16/12*99.5625e6 = 132.75 exactly. The 16 B/cmd read path needs no port widening; only the WRITE path does, because the write side is the one at 2 B/cmd today. Port width only becomes the binding constraint once the cadence reaches 3 pclk/cmd.
